Write a C++ function that takes a reference to a std::string containing markdown formatted text and returns a std::string containing html formatted text.

Response:

|-\|/-\|/-\|/-\|/-\|/-\|/-\|/-\|/-\|/-\|/-\|/-\|/-\|/-\|/-\|/-\|/-\|/-\|/-\|/-\|/-\|/-\|/-\|/-\|/-\|/-\|/-\|/-\|/-\|/-\|/-\|/-\|/-\|/-\|/-\|/- Certainly! Below is a C++ function that takes a reference to a `std::string` containing markdown formatted text and returns a `std::string` containing HTML formatted text. This function uses a simple mapping of markdown to HTML tags for demonstration purposes. In a real-world scenario, you would use a more comprehensive library like `markdown` or `pandoc` for more accurate conversion.

```cpp
#include <string>
#include <iostream>

// A simple mapping of markdown to HTML tags
std::map<std::string, std::string> markdownToHtml = {
    {"# ", "<h1>"},
    {"## ", "<h2>"},
    {"### ", "<h3>"},
    {"#### ", "<h4>"},
    {"##### ", "<h5>"},
    {"###### ", "<h6>"},
    {"<p>", "<p>"},
    {"<ul>", "<ul>"},
    {"<li>", "<li>"},
    {"<ol>", "<ol>"},
    {"<br>", "<br/>"},
    {"<a>", "<a href=\""},
    {"</a>", "\">"},
    {"<img>", "<img src=\""},
    {"</img>", "\" alt=\"\" />"},
    // Add more mappings as needed
};

std::string markdownToHtml(std::string markdownText) {
    std::string htmlText;
    std::string::size_type pos = 0;
    std::string::size_type oldPos = 0;

    while ((pos = markdownText.find(" ", oldPos)) != std::string::npos) {
        std::string token = markdownText.substr(oldPos, pos - oldPos);
        std::string htmlToken = markdownToHtml[token];
        htmlText += htmlToken;
        oldPos = pos + 1;
    }

    // Handle the last token
    std::string token = markdownText.substr(oldPos);
    std::string htmlToken = markdownToHtml[token];
    htmlText += htmlToken;

    return htmlText;
}

int main() {
    std::string markdown = "## Markdown Title\n\n### Subheading\n\n<p>This is a paragraph.</p>\n\n<ol>\n<li>First item</li>\n<li>Second item</li>\n</ol>\n\n<a href=\"http://example.com\">Link to example</a>\n\n<img src=\"image.jpg\" alt=\"Example Image\" />";
    std::string html = markdownToHtml(markdown);
    std::cout << "Markdown:\n" << markdown << "\n\nHTML:\n" << html << std::endl;
    return 0;
}
```

This function uses a simple mapping of markdown tags to HTML tags. It iterates through the markdown text, replacing each markdown tag with its corresponding HTML tag. Note that this is a very basic implementation and may not handle all markdown features. For a more robust solution, consider using a library like `markdown` or `pandoc`.

